{"builder":"Goal: one pull request that meets both of these lines:\n1. \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\n2. \"npm test passes\"\n\nSteps:\n1. Read src/config.js, src/app.js, test/config.test.js and README.md. Right now loadConfig returns JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8')), so bad JSON throws a SyntaxError. src/app.js catches that, prints `bad config: <message>` and exits 1, and README.md describes that behaviour.\n2. In src/config.js, change loadConfig(path) so that if JSON.parse throws, it returns {} instead of throwing. Put only the JSON.parse call inside the try. The readFileSync call stays outside it, so a missing or unreadable file still throws as it does today. Keep the function exported under the same name and signature, and leave valid-JSON parsing unchanged.\n3. Update test/config.test.js. The existing test 'bad JSON throws' expects a SyntaxError, which contradicts line 1, so change it to assert that loadConfig on a file containing `{port: 9000` returns {} (deepEqual). Keep the 'reads a config' test. Optionally add a test that a missing file still throws.\n4. Note the side effect: src/app.js merges loadConfig's result over DEFAULTS, so a malformed config.json will now start the app with the defaults and no error message. Update the README.md sentence about invalid JSON so it describes the new behaviour. Do not add more behaviour than line 1 asks for. In the PR description, say plainly that this change makes a typo in config.json silent (the app starts with defaults and does not report which file is broken or where). The issue's underlying need is to tell people what to fix, so a reviewer may want a follow-up that logs or reports the parse error.\n5. Run `npm test` at the repository root and make sure it passes with no failing tests.\n6. Commit and open the pull request.","verifier":"Run every check at the pull request's head, from the repository root, after `npm install` if the repo needs it (it has no dependencies, so this may be a no-op).\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\nRun: write a file containing invalid JSON, e.g. `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > \"$d/config.json\"`, then run `node --input-type=module -e \"import { loadConfig } from './src/config.js'; const r = loadConfig(process.argv[1]); console.log(JSON.stringify(r));\" \"$d/config.json\"`. Also read src/config.js to confirm loadConfig is the function in that file that does this.\nPASS: the command does not throw and prints `{}`, meaning loadConfig returned an empty object for the malformed JSON, and the behaviour is in loadConfig in src/config.js.\nFAIL: the command throws (for example a SyntaxError), prints anything other than an empty object, or the behaviour lives somewhere other than loadConfig in src/config.js.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: `npm test` at the repository root.\nPASS: npm test completes and reports no failing tests.\nFAIL: npm test reports any failing test or errors out.\n\nAdvisory (not PASS/FAIL conditions):\n- Confirm valid JSON still parses: a file containing `{\"port\": 9000}` should give `{\"port\":9000}`.\n- Check whether a missing file still throws (e.g. call loadConfig on a nonexistent path). The line covers only the JSON.parse error, so swallowing read errors too would go beyond it.\n- Check that `npm test` exits with status 0 and that test/config.test.js no longer expects a SyntaxError for bad JSON.\n- Run `node src/app.js \"$d/config.json\"`. It will now likely print `listening on 8080` instead of `bad config: ...`. That fits the expect line, but it means a typo is now silent, and the issue's stated need was to tell people which file is broken and where. Note whether README.md and the PR description acknowledge this."}